      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Compression Ratio Continuity (1 : 13.9) Across the Matrix</h2>
        <p>
          A vital structural principle in public administration economics is the <strong>Compression Ratio</strong>—the proportion between the entry floor wage (Level 1, Cell 1) and the maximum executive compensation (Level 18, Cabinet Secretary).
        </p>
        <p>
          While the 1st Pay Commission maintained a wide ratio of 1 : 36.4, successive commissions gradually compressed this spread to foster internal administrative cohesion. The 7th CPC established a ratio of <strong>1 : 13.88</strong> (₹18,000 at Level 1 to ₹2,50,000 at Level 18).
        </p>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <p>
            <strong>Under the 8th CPC (2.28x Working Factor):</strong> Minimum Pay = ₹41,040 | Apex Level 18 Pay = ₹5,70,000 | <strong>Compression Ratio = 1 : 13.88</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Under the 8th CPC (2.86x Maximum Demand):</strong> Minimum Pay = ₹51,480 | Apex Level 18 Pay = ₹7,15,000 | <strong>Compression Ratio = 1 : 13.88</strong>
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Preserving this established 1 : 13.9 ratio ensures institutional balance between entry-level operational support cadres, mid-level supervisory officers, and the executive policy leadership of the country.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Impact on State Bureaucracy (All India Services Cadres)</h2>
        <p>
          Because officers of the Indian Administrative Service (IAS), Indian Police Service (IPS), and Indian Forest Service (IFS) serve directly within state secretariats and district collectorates across <strong>Tamil Nadu</strong>, <strong>Uttar Pradesh</strong>, <strong>Maharashtra</strong>, <strong>Rajasthan</strong>, <strong>Bihar</strong>, and <strong>Karnataka</strong>, Levels 10 through 18 apply across state administrations without delay.
        </p>
        <p>
          State finance departments adopt these central pay levels directly for All India Services personnel, which subsequently informs the pay scales of state administrative service officers promoted into IAS/IPS cadres. Consequently, the Level 10 to 18 matrix serves as the primary compensation framework for senior administrative leadership across both the Union and State Governments.
        </p>
